That's true, but that particular heroic effort is largely wasted from
the standpoint of players. Face it, the core NetHack has changed so
little in 10 years that the 15-year old machine you ran it on in 1997
may as well keep running that same version until its motherboard
shorts out.

Nowadays, it would make a lot more sense to move to GNU configure and
MinGW. That would produce two configurations that modern development
tools support and which will still handle legacy hardware going back
to 2000 and before. Also, you could plop the project down into
Eclipse, no sweat. It actually builds just fine in Eclipse (using the
Unix sys tree) but the nonstandard build structure is a hindrance. I'd
like to be able to get the Windows app building in Eclipse with MinGW
(for easy debugging among other things) but the whole makefile
structure is wrong for that.



